WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
The term human level performance is sometimes used casually in research articles.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.800
But let me show you how we can define it a bit more precisely, and in particular,

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.000
use the definition of the phrase human level performance

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.000
that is most useful for helping you drive progress in your machine learning project.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:25.000
So remember from our last video that one of the uses of this phrase human level error

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
is that it gives us a way of estimating Bayes' error,

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:35.000
whether it's the best possible error any function could either now or in the future ever, ever achieve.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
So bearing that in mind, let's look at a medical image classification example.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Let's say that you want to look at a radiology image like this

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.000
and make a diagnosis classification decision.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.000
And suppose that a typical human, untrained human, achieves 3% error on this task,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:02.000
a typical doctor, maybe a typical radiologist doctor, achieves 1% error,

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.000
an experienced doctor does even better, 0.7% error,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.000
and a team of experienced doctors, that is, if you get a team of experienced doctors,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
and have them all look at the image and discuss and debate the image,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.000
together their consensus opinion achieves 0.5% error.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.000
So the question I want to pose to you is how should you define human level error?

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Is human level error 3%, 1%, 0.7%, or 0.5%?

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Feel free to pause this video to think about it if you wish.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.000
And to answer that question, I would urge you to bear in mind that

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.000
one of the most useful ways to think of human error is as a proxy or an estimate for Bayes' error.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.000
So please be free to pause this video to think about it for a while if you wish.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.000
But here's how I would define human level error,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.000
which is if you want a proxy or an estimate for Bayes' error,

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:06.000
then given that a team of experienced doctors discussing and debating can achieve 0.5% error,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
we know that Bayes' error is less than or equal to 0.5%.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.000
So because some system, a team of these doctors can achieve 0.5% error,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.000
so by definition, the theoretically optimal error has got to be 0.5% or lower.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.000
We don't know how much better it is, maybe there's an even larger team of even more experienced doctors that could do even better,

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:36.000
so maybe it's even a little bit better than 0.5%, but we know the optimal error cannot be higher than 0.5%.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:43.000
So what I would do in this setting is use 0.5% as our estimate for Bayes' error,

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.000
so I would define human level performance as 0.5%,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:55.000
at least if you're hoping to use human level error in the analysis of bias invariance as we saw in the last video.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.000
Now for the purpose of publishing a research paper or for the purpose of deploying a system,

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.000
maybe there's a different definition of human level error that you can use,

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.000
which is so long as you surpass the performance of a typical doctor,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.000
that seems like maybe a very useful result to have accomplished,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.000
and maybe surpassing a single radiologist or single doctor's performance

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:21.000
might mean the system is good enough to deploy in some context.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:28.000
So maybe the takeaway from this is to be clear about what your purpose is in defining the term human level error,

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:36.000
and if it is to show that you can surpass a single human and therefore argue for deploying your system in some context,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:44.000
maybe this is the appropriate definition, but if your goal is the proxy for Bayes' error, then this is the appropriate definition.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.000
To see why this matters, let's look at an error analysis example.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:59.240
Let's say for a medical imaging diagnosis example that your training error is 5% and your death error is 6%.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:05.240
And in the example from the previous slide, human level performance,

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.240
and I'm going to think of this as proxy for Bayes' error,

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:16.990
depending on whether you define it as a typical doctor's performance or an experienced doctor or a team of doctors,

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:23.990
you would have either 1% or 0.7% or 0.5% for this.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:31.990
And remember also our definitions from the previous video that this gap between Bayes' error or the estimate of Bayes' error and training error

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:35.990
us calling that a measure of the avoidable bias,

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:41.990
and this as a measure or an estimate of how much of a variance problem you have in your learning algorithm.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:48.490
So in this first example, whichever of these choices you make,

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:58.490
the measure of avoidable bias will be something like 4%, so it will be somewhat between I guess 4% if you take that to 4.5%,

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:06.020
if you use 0.5%, whereas this is 1%.

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:12.020
So in this example, I would say it doesn't really matter which of the definitions of human level error you use,

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:20.020
whether you use the typical doctor's error or the single experience doctor's error or the team of experienced doctor's error,

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:27.020
this, whether this is 4% or 4.5%, this is clearly bigger than the variance problem.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:34.020
And so in this case, you should focus on bias reduction techniques, such as train a bigger network.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:42.020
Now let's look at a second example. Let's say your training error is 1% and your depth error is 5%.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:49.020
Then again, it doesn't really matter, it seems a bit academic whether the human level performance is 1% or 0.7% or 0.5%,

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:59.020
because whichever of these definitions you use, your measure of avoidable bias will be, I guess, somewhere between 0% if you use that to 0.5%,

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:04.020
that's the gap between the human level performance and your training error, whereas this gap is 4%.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:09.020
So this 4% is going to be much bigger than the avoidable bias either way.

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:13.020
And so, you know, this suggests you should focus on variance reduction techniques,

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:16.020
such as regularization or getting a bigger training set.

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:22.020
But where it really matters will be if your training error is 0.7%, so you're doing really well now,

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:26.020
and your depth error is 0.8%.

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:34.020
In this case, it really matters that you use your estimate for base error as 0.5%,

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:40.460
because in this case, your measure of how much avoidable bias you have is 0.2%,

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:48.460
which is twice as big as your measure for your variance, which is just 0.1%.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:54.460
And so this suggests that maybe both the bias and variance are both problems, but maybe the avoidable bias is a bit bigger of a problem.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:07:00.460
And in this example, 0.5%, as we discussed on the previous slide, was the best measure of base error,

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:03.460
because a team of human doctors could achieve that performance.

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:11.460
If you use 0.7 as your proxy for base error, you would have estimated the avoidable bias as, you know, pretty much 0%,

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:16.460
and you might have missed that you actually should try to do better on your training set.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:26.700
So I hope this gives a sense also of why making progress in the machine learning problem gets harder as you achieve or as you approach human level performance.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:34.700
In this example, once you've approached 0.7% error, unless you're very careful about estimating base error,

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:41.700
you might not know how far away you are from base error, and therefore how much you should be trying to reduce avoidable bias.

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:48.700
In fact, if all you knew was that a single typical doctor achieves 1% error,

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:53.700
and it might be very difficult to know if you should be trying to fit your training set even better.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:58.700
And this problem arose only when you're doing very well on your problem already,

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:03.700
only when you're doing, you know, 0.7%, 0.8%, really close to human level performance.

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:09.700
Whereas in the two examples on the left, when you were further away from human level performance,

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:12.700
it was easier to tell if you should focus on bias or variance.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:16.700
So this is maybe an illustration of why as you approach human level performance,

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:19.700
it is actually harder to tease out the bias and the variance effects.

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:24.700
And therefore why progress on your machine learning project just gets harder as you're doing really well.

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:27.700
So just to summarize what we've talked about,

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:32.700
if you're trying to understand bias and variance where you have an estimate of human level error,

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:38.700
for a task that humans can do quite well, you can use human level error as a proxy or as an approximation,

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.700
for Bayes' error.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:54.600
And so the difference between your estimate of Bayes' error tells you how much avoidable bias is a problem,

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.600
how much avoidable bias there is, and the difference between training error and dev error,

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.600
that tells you how much variance is a problem,

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:06.600
as whether your algorithm is able to generalize from the training set to the dev set.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:11.600
And the big difference between our discussion here and what we saw in earlier course

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:15.600
was that instead of comparing training error to 0%,

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:22.330
and just calling that the estimate of the bias,

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:28.330
in contrast in this video we have a more nuanced analysis in which there is no particular expectation

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:31.330
that you should get 0% error.

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:37.330
Because sometimes Bayes' error is non-zero and sometimes it's just not possible for anything to do better

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:39.330
than a certain threshold of error.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:45.740
And so in the earlier course we were measuring training error

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:52.740
and seeing how much bigger training error was than 0 and just using that to try to understand how big our bias is.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:57.740
And that turns out to work just fine for problems where Bayes' error is nearly 0%,

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:03.740
such as recognizing cats humans are near perfect for that, so Bayes' error is also near perfect for that.

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:06.740
So that actually works okay when Bayes' error is nearly 0.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:11.740
But for problems where the data is noisy, like speech recognition on very noisy audio,

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:15.740
where it's just impossible sometimes to hear what was said and to get the correct transcription,

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:22.740
for problems like that having a better estimate for Bayes' error can help you better estimate avoidable bias and variance

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:28.740
and therefore make better decisions on whether to focus on bias reduction tactics or on variance reduction tactics.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:35.740
So to recap, having an estimate of human level performance gives you an estimate of Bayes' error

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:41.740
and this allows you to more quickly make decisions as to whether you should focus on trying to reduce the bias

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:44.740
or trying to reduce the variance of your algorithm.

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:49.740
And these techniques will tend to work well until you surpass human level performance,

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:57.740
whereupon you might no longer have a good estimate of Bayes' error that still helps you make this decision really clearly.

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:02.740
Now one of the exciting developments in deep learning has been that for more and more tasks,

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:05.740
we're actually able to surpass human level performance.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:11.740
In the next video, let's talk more about the process of surpassing human level performance.
